          "text": "{\n  \"locale\": \"en\",\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"An organization can endure despite replacement of its constituent elements when it continually reorganizes those elements and maintains its mode of operation, rather than depending on every individual element remaining unchanged.\",\n      \"supportFragmentIds\": [\n        \"F-1d3a288563890410a103383b6f1c33d31e56465a2ce7390c59105b4bee413edc\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"This endurance is presented as a dynamic capacity: when parts change, remaining structures can readjust; when prior pathways cease to work, new pathways can gradually be established; and organizational arrangements can adapt to environmental change.\",\n      \"supportFragmentIds\": [\n        \"F-1d3a288563890410a103383b6f1c33d31e56465a2ce7390c59105b4bee413edc\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The fragment illustrates this with a city: buildings, roads, facilities, population, and industries may change, while the city continues through its ability to restore overall functions such as transport, resources, production, exchange, and order.\",\n      \"supportFragmentIds\": [\n        \"F-1d3a288563890410a103383b6f1c33d31e56465a2ce7390c59105b4bee413edc\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sourceLanguages\": [\n    \"zh-Hans\"\n  ],\n  \"status\": \"supported\",\n  \"boundary\": \"The supplied fragment supports a general account of continuity through ongoing reorganization and adaptation, illustrated by a city. It does not specify necessary institutional mechanisms, thresholds of replacement, or criteria for determining when an organization has ceased to be the same organization.\"\n}"
        }
